{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang","id":827135,"title":"LGBTQ Slang","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-02-09T15:31:17-08:00","id":827135,"title":"LGBTQ Slang","content":"So, a number of folks here are active in their LGBTQ communities.<br /> I was wondering in your communities what is the non pejorative slang for people who are not LGBTQ?<br /> I ask because I am writing a script and I am looking to this for some inspiration.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer","name":"omenseer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"987"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-09T17:01:29-08:00","id":827167,"title":"","content":"While not 'slang,' an important term to recognize is cis/cisgender; it refers to anyone who isn't trans or non-binary, and neatly solves the pickle of describing that without the unfortunate implications inherent in calling such folks 'normal.' You'll sometimes see described as cishets - cis individuals who are heterosexual.<br /> <br /> Many folks don't bother saying the entire LGBTQA+ acronym out loud, because it's a bit of a mouthful; queer is concise enough as an umbrella term, though it does have a lingering reputation from its days as an insult.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T13:13:26-08:00","id":827732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827167#post827167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Many folks don't bother saying the entire LGBTQA+ acronym out loud, because it's a bit of a mouthful; queer is concise enough as an umbrella term, though it does have a lingering reputation from its days as an insult.</div> </div> </div> </div>No kidding? I thought the word had been robbed of negative connotations.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/271-jimb","name":"JimB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar271_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T13:15:42-08:00","id":827736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827732#post827732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No kidding? I thought the word had been robbed of negative connotations.</div> </div> </div> </div>The LGBTQ community is more than happy to call itself queer; it's what the Q stands for!<br /> <br /> But if someone sees a gay couple on the street and screams &quot;fuck you, queers!&quot; then obviously that's not great.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T19:29:03-08:00","id":827931,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JimB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827732#post827732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No kidding? I thought the word had been robbed of negative connotations.</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is, the LGBTQIA community is made up of individuals. Some of those individuals reclaim the term for themselves, while others can still be deeply hurt by it. Since there's no way of knowing if someone will be hurt by it or not, it's best not to use it for anyone who hasn't specifically said that they are ok with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T20:08:23-08:00","id":827945,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827931#post827931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing is, the LGBTQIA community is made up of individuals. Some of those individuals reclaim the term for themselves, while others can still be deeply hurt by it. Since there's no way of knowing if someone will be hurt by it or not, it's best not to use it for anyone who hasn't specifically said that they are ok with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thing is, that's true for almost every word when talking about someone's identity. My work colleague gets very defensive or straight-out aggressive when someone calls him gay. We really haven't asked why, we've just stopped referring to his sexual orientation. He's fine talking about his boyfriend and calling him like that though.<br /> <br /> On the other end of the spectrum, a few gay friends of mine don't even mind when I use &quot;gay&quot; as a pronoun or as their name &quot;Gay'll show you the way&quot;, for example.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I still feel like any word relating to one person's identity might be taken wildly differently.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Glorious Useless-Post Prana<br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Cost: 2</span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">m; </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mins: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Linguistics 1, Essence 1 </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Type: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Simple</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Keywords: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Mute</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Duration: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Instant</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Prerequisite Charms: </span><span style=\"font-family:MercuryTextG2\">Ten-Characters Methodology</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2589-magnum-opus","name":"Magnum Opus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2589_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"821"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T20:54:17-08:00","id":827966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magnum Opus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827945#post827945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thing is, that's true for almost every word when talking about someone's identity. My work colleague gets very defensive or straight-out aggressive when someone calls him gay. We really haven't asked why, we've just stopped referring to his sexual orientation. He's fine talking about his boyfriend and calling him like that though.<br /> <br /> On the other end of the spectrum, a few gay friends of mine don't even mind when I use &amp;quot;gay&amp;quot; as a pronoun or as their name &amp;quot;Gay'll show you the way&amp;quot;, for example.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I still feel like any word relating to one person's identity might be taken wildly differently.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but that goes doubly so for words with a history of being used prejoratively. You can't avoid accidentally offending people from time to time, but you <i>can</i> avoid using terms that are frequently used to intentionally offend people.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-10T22:45:05-08:00","id":828022,"title":"","content":"Heteronormative isn't slang, but says a lot. It's the idea that being hetero is the norm, so framing everything in the world in that context. Words shape our worldview, so how you frame things matters.<br /> <br /> Erasure is a pretty big one too, both for trans and bi people. And... those happen from both cishet and LGBTQ communities. I'm not bi, but I know quite a few, and there's this perception that paints bi people either in a very broad negative light or just being het or not on who they are in a relationship with currently. For trans, there's that even events where trans people were important, they are left out or ignored. Stonewall was a really big example of this (though in fairness, we weren't the only ones who weren't given the credit as a community we deserve).<br /> <br /> I think the main problem here is... even if something has more a clinical nature, when referring to the majority, it's treated like it's a slur because &quot;We're normal. We don't need that.&quot; See how some people react to cis.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T01:10:55-08:00","id":828056,"title":"","content":"I've seen &quot;normies&quot; used by some to refer to cishet people, as a somewhat pejorative reaction against that kind of attitude.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Ultimate Jade-Screened Exalted Scholar, Savant of the Immaculate Texts, No Moon Scholar<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1304239-my-next-campaign-tdhr-peach\" target=\"_blank\">THDR : Future Dragon-Blooded campaign</a>, and other links.<br /> If you like Ex3, you can support <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/holdenshearer\" target=\"_blank\">Holden</a> on Patreon!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/541-letipex","name":"LeTipex","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar541_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"902"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T01:21:07-08:00","id":828063,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=827167#post827167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> . . .Many folks don't bother saying the entire LGBTQA+ acronym out loud, because it's a bit of a mouthful; queer is concise enough as an umbrella term, though it does have a lingering reputation from its days as an insult.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm curious - what does adding &quot;Q&quot; (or &quot;queer&quot;) to the gay rights acronym add, conceptually speaking? I remember when it went from being BiGLM to LGBT . . . which made sense to me. The change reflected an increased concern for the rights/problems of transgendered individuals, and brought another group under the movement's umbrella. <br /> <br /> Is there a similar block of individuals, or set of issues, reflected in the new LGBTQ name? It just seems redundant, having both &quot;Q&quot; and &quot;LGB&quot; - sort of like saying the &quot;flying bird flew&quot; or the &quot;swimming fish swam&quot;. And despite asking around, no one seems to have a good answer (at least in my pool of contacts). If anyone has a moment, could you fill me in?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T01:32:07-08:00","id":828068,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828063#post828063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious - what does adding &amp;quot;Q&amp;quot; (or &amp;quot;queer&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> to the gay rights acronym add, conceptually speaking? I remember when it went from being BiGLM to LGBT . . . which made sense to me. The change reflected an increased concern for the rights/problems of transgendered individuals, and brought another group under the movement's umbrella. <br /> <br /> Is there a similar block of individuals, or set of issues, reflected in the new LGBTQ name? It just seems redundant, having both &amp;quot;Q&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;LGB&amp;quot; - sort of like saying the &amp;quot;flying bird flew&amp;quot; or the &amp;quot;swimming fish swam&amp;quot;. And despite asking around, no one seems to have a good answer (at least in my pool of contacts). If anyone has a moment, could you fill me in?</div> </div> </div> </div>'Queer' covers everyone who doesn't get their own letter in the acronym; pansexuals, nonbinary, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T06:41:30-08:00","id":828188,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828063#post828063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious - what does adding &amp;quot;Q&amp;quot; (or &amp;quot;queer&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> to the gay rights acronym add, conceptually speaking? I remember when it went from being BiGLM to LGBT . . . which made sense to me. The change reflected an increased concern for the rights/problems of transgendered individuals, and brought another group under the movement's umbrella. <br /> <br /> Is there a similar block of individuals, or set of issues, reflected in the new LGBTQ name? It just seems redundant, having both &amp;quot;Q&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;LGB&amp;quot; - sort of like saying the &amp;quot;flying bird flew&amp;quot; or the &amp;quot;swimming fish swam&amp;quot;. And despite asking around, no one seems to have a good answer (at least in my pool of contacts). If anyone has a moment, could you fill me in?</div> </div> </div> </div>Q is an umbrella term for people who exist outside the gender, sex, and/or sexual binaries, but don't fit under the categories Lesbian, Gay, Bi, or Trans (or Intersex or Asexual/Agender, which are now included in the acronym as well). There are as many gender identities as there are people, and it's important that people have their identities acknowledged rather than erased. At the same time, there comes a point where the acronym just gets to be alphabet soup. Adding Q is somewhat of a compromise between those two conflicting necessities. It acknowledges that a wide variety exists beyond the other letters, while keeping the acronym from ballooning out of control.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T06:42:41-08:00","id":828190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828068#post828068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 'Queer' covers everyone who doesn't get their own letter in the acronym; pansexuals, nonbinary, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah, this. I generally use LGBTQQIA because that second Q is really important. It stands for &quot;Questioning&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T19:54:14-08:00","id":828566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828022#post828022\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Heteronormative isn't slang, but says a lot. It's the idea that being hetero is the norm, so framing everything in the world in that context. Words shape our worldview, so how you frame things matters.<br /> <br /> (. . .)<br /> <br /> I think the main problem here is... even if something has more a clinical nature, when referring to the majority, it's treated like it's a slur because &quot;We're normal. We don't need that.&quot; See how some people react to cis.</div> </div> </div> </div>As a straight guy, I'm not wild about the connotations that &quot;heteronormative&quot; or &quot;cis-gendered&quot; are picking up. Particularly in places like the student protests at Brown University, they're being used as synonyms for &quot;misogynist&quot;, &quot;knuckle dragger&quot;, &quot;gay bashing&quot;, &quot;Neanderthal&quot; . . .<br /> <br /> You are making a valid point, though. There's a fairly well-known essay, &quot;Body Rites of the Nacirema&quot;, that's been floating around for a couple of decades now. A description of conventional American society told using clinical/technical terminology, it really lays into anthropologists/sociologists for &quot;othering&quot; the societies they study.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T21:30:27-08:00","id":828592,"title":"","content":"The point of the term cisgender is to have a way to describe people who are not trans that doesn't carry implications of trans being abnormal. I guess it sucks that some people use it in a negative context. Sucks when people use trans in a negative context too. But we have to have some kind of language to use, and the Latin prefixes for &quot;next to&quot; and &quot;across from&quot; seem relatively harmless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-02-11T23:46:22-08:00","id":828630,"title":"","content":"That's pretty much my feeling as well, Charlaquin. And it also feels familiar due to my background in chemistry.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T14:40:40-07:00","id":867827,"title":"","content":"I have a question: is there a reason why I see stuff like LGBTIAUQ+ much more often than the acronym QUILTBAG? I think it's neat and much easier to use in spoken conversation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/7019410/Images/Ex3%20KS%20Badge.gif\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":1866,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1866-ophion","user":"Ophion","date":". 04-18-2016, 05:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1866-ophion","name":"Ophion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T14:56:24-07:00","id":867839,"title":"","content":"I think that's mostly because while QUILTBAG is easier to say, the first is more likely to be roughly understood without you needing to take far more time to explain what you mean by that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T10:24:28-07:00","id":868543,"title":"","content":"QUILTBAG has an unfortunate resemblance to &quot;douchebag&quot;.<br /> For a non-pejorative term for non-LGBTQIA+ I usually hear cis-het.<br /> As a cis-het, I'm fine with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:lucida sans unicode\">Bearer of the legacy of Trauma Bear<br /> Need a dice-roller? Check out <a href=\"http://bit.ly/dicemat\" target=\"_blank\">Dicemat</a>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/43-fata-ku","name":"Fata-Ku","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar43_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2240"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T11:59:39-07:00","id":868632,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828566#post828566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a straight guy, I'm not wild about the connotations that &quot;heteronormative&quot; or &quot;cis-gendered&quot; are picking up. Particularly in places like the student protests at Brown University, they're being used as synonyms for &quot;misogynist&quot;, &quot;knuckle dragger&quot;, &quot;gay bashing&quot;, &quot;Neanderthal&quot; . . .</div> </div> </div> </div>+1 to that.<br /> <br /> My main gripe with that word, and its more blaise/agressive/tumblr usage is that it confuses a descriptive term with a prescriptive term. Or, more insidiously, it uses heteronormative where heteronormal is the proper expression.<br /> <br /> &quot;Normal&quot;, fundamentally, means nothing more than average, or mean, or center of the distribution. Heteronormal, as a descriptive term, is perfectly fine. A relative majority of people in the world are heterosexual, so if you take a grand average over all those people, the norm will be, not surprisingly, hetero, and barring massive changes, this is not likely to move. Perhaps a couple notches down the Kinsey scale, but not to the middle or the other side.<br /> <br /> Normative, on the other hand, carries massive connotations of active use, of doing, of trying to move a situation towards that. One, could, for example, attempt to make society more or less heteronormative.<br /> <br /> Sadly, most uses I have come across use the word &quot;normative&quot;, with all its negative connotations, when they ought to use normal.<br /> <br /> For my own two cents, I am heterosexual, and male, and have absolutely zero issues with sexual/gender preferences that do not overlap with mine. Go out and make someone happy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5238,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","user":"Herbert_West","date":". 04-19-2016, 03:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T14:42:36-07:00","id":868784,"title":"","content":"But heteronormative is being used - generally obviously individuals misuse words all the time - correctly for what people are trying to generally say.<br /> <br /> Nobody is arguing about where or not any given sexual or gender is a minority of the population in a statistical sense, but in a sociological sense (which a matter of possession of social power, not solely population size). Most groups advocating for sexual and gender base rights/protections want to make society less heteronomative; not make society less heteronormal.<br /> <br /> The connotations of active use is completely intentional. There are individuals/organizations actively discriminating, and institutional elements actively discriminating, to make society more heteronormative.<br /> <br /> Obviously in casual conversation people don't type up full academic essays, and they allow connotations to carry a lot of intent. Getting nitpicky rarely helps. Just like it does no good to constantly correct people saying, &quot;I have a theory!&quot; when they really mean, &quot;I have a hypothesis!&quot; We all know that in casual non-academic settings the word theory is a lot looser in usage.<br /> <br /> And, &quot; Go out and make someone happy. &quot; this doesn't happen for a lot of people because society is currently too heteronormative to allow it. People are being actively discriminated against in a fashion that impedes - either partially or fully - their ability to do what they want with other fully consenting adults to make everyone involved happy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T15:17:06-07:00","id":868811,"title":"","content":"I was going to reply, but got called away to try and handle things, and then Heavy Arms coming in and saying pretty much everything on that topic I wanted to. High five!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T17:53:10-07:00","id":868928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herbert_West</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=868632#post868632\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or, more insidiously, it uses heteronormative where heteronormal is the proper expression.</div> </div> </div> </div>In practice, &quot;heteronormal&quot; would reinforce the notion that non-heterosexuals are abnormal.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted.<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1120-eric-minton","name":"Eric Minton","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1120_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2000"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T18:42:45-07:00","id":868973,"title":"","content":"I think there's a disconnect there between academic and general language.<br /> <br /> In an academic/demographic sense, our species is heteronormal: the majority of humans are heterosexual or heterosexual leaning along that spectrum.<br /> <br /> In general conversation though, people don't mean the statistical definition of normal. Normal is usually closer to, &quot;expected,&quot; rather than, &quot;average.&quot; Nobody - in casual conversation - is going to call out people outside of various statistically average demographics &quot;abnormal,&quot; as long as they're still expected. Most people don't even know though the distributions of, say, blood types to know what's average or not. People know some combinations are more rare than others, but if you're sitting around chatting and it comes up that someone in your group is A- you're not going to comment on how abnormal they are (A- is ~3% of the human population). It's just a fact about them. You might be a rarity to a hematologist, but even most of them aren't going to be weird about it.<br /> <br /> When people want to push for inclusive language, that's the goal. For being whatever identity you have to be part of the &quot;normal&quot; in the sense of the expected. A trans-man is no more noteworthy to what is normal by society's standards than having A- blood (maybe less so due to the need for blood donations as a practical matter) is the goal.<br /> <br /> That's why those of us knowledgeable about the academic lingo need to be aware of how people who aren't are going to interpret things. As I said before, trying to correct common language doesn't work. Common language beats academic language in societal importance and always will. It doesn't matter if heteronormal is a perfectly neutral academic term (so is retarded as a different example). Using such words needs to be done with care for precisely the reason Eric said: people will read them under the context of common language not academic neutral. And in common language making one thing normal, means something else is abnormal; and abnormal things are vastly more likely to be the subject of discrimination.<br /> <br /> Though this little aside feels like it's getting away from the point of the thread, I'll try to tie it back by saying that this is all core to a lot of current slang in the communities in question. Though that qualifier is also important. There isn't one LGBTQ community with one slang. Slang varies with location and so on. Terms that might be cool in Miami aren't necessarily terms that are cool in NYC. But there is still a common cultural pressure behind those slang variations. There's a desire for both group identity that goes with any sub-culture, and the desire for acceptance by the greater culture around them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T19:25:16-07:00","id":869003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=868973#post868973\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">IThough this little aside feels like it's getting away from the point of the thread, I'll try to tie it back by saying that this is all core to a lot of current slang in the communities in question. Though that qualifier is also important. There isn't one LGBTQ community with one slang. Slang varies with location and so on. Terms that might be cool in Miami aren't necessarily terms that are cool in NYC. But there is still a common cultural pressure behind those slang variations. There's a desire for both group identity that goes with any sub-culture, and the desire for acceptance by the greater culture around them.</div> </div> </div> </div>And what is acceptable is often very much a personal issue as well. I've known people who are ok with other people using queer to describe themselves, but do not want it for their personal identity. Language is complex, and honestly, there is no &quot;right&quot; language or vocabulary (assuming we're not talking slurs).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T20:35:03-07:00","id":869056,"title":"","content":"Absolutely. I was just focusing on communities because slang is a communal construct.<br /> <br /> I'm personally fine with being called het in whatever form, even if my personal identity is more complex than that, I'm close enough that it isn't worth the time and effort for me to sweat the difference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T11:36:17-07:00","id":877927,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828566#post828566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a straight guy, I'm not wild about the connotations that &quot;heteronormative&quot; or &quot;cis-gendered&quot; are picking up. Particularly in places like the student protests at Brown University, they're being used as synonyms for &quot;misogynist&quot;, &quot;knuckle dragger&quot;, &quot;gay bashing&quot;, &quot;Neanderthal&quot; . . .</div> </div> </div> </div>Heteronormative should have negative connotations. It doesn't say anything negative about heterosexual people, it says something negative about society at large. Cisgender is not a negative term, though sometimes people can take it negatively when their privilege is being pointed out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828566#post828566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You are making a valid point, though. There's a fairly well-known essay, &quot;Body Rites of the Nacirema&quot;, that's been floating around for a couple of decades now. A description of conventional American society told using clinical/technical terminology, it really lays into anthropologists/sociologists for &quot;othering&quot; the societies they study.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is that what you took from that essay? Huh ... I took it as an eye opener that anybody's society can look weird to someone who isn't used to it, that NOBODY'S culture is the default.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/avatar/20th%20Anniversary/2013~Mage%20The%20Ascension/img.php?h=120&amp;w=120&amp;img=1~M20.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1018-ophidimancer","name":"Ophidimancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1018_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"596"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T13:33:23-07:00","id":878050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ophidimancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=877927#post877927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Heteronormative should have negative connotations. It doesn't say anything negative about heterosexual people, it says something negative about society at large. Cisgender is not a negative term, though sometimes people can take it negatively when their privilege is being pointed out.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Is that what you took from that essay? Huh ... I took it as an eye opener that anybody's society can look weird to someone who isn't used to it, that NOBODY'S culture is the default.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally I don't use any terms either way. Cisgendered does not mean normal in my book. My view of their normality depends on how they treat people around them, and whether or not they feel that they have to live at the expense of others (by denying them their rights, taking advantage of them economically, and so on). Accordingly, an abusive trans girlfriend clearly deviates from what I consider normal (hint: it's because she is abusive, not because she is trans - I've had crushes on trans women before).<br /> <br /> As for privilege, I've had mine pointed out disastrously a few weeks back. All I said was, in the presence of company that was discussing politics, that I would side with anarcho-communist groups but I have my reservations because of rumors of police infiltration in said groups.<br /> <br /> Someone pointed out my and the anarchists' privilege, since we are white males. As a clinically depressed, sexually adventurous, socially disadvantaged, ethnic and religious minority (English white is not the same as Italian white, Greek white, Turkish white and so on) that has suffered a LOT in life due to other people. She apologised after I explained my situation and she pointed out that since she opposes injustice, her privilege does not matter. It was very unpleasant, having to justify my existence to someone (wealthier, healthier, happier than myself).<br /> <br /> Call me old fashioned but I prefer class-based revolutionary politics. The fact that this, otherwise lovely person, opposes the patriarchy but not capitalism (as her views emerged from our discussion) led me to the realization that we didn't share the same goals. Feminism is front and center for my ideology, but a fully inclusive capitalist society that still attacks third world countries for resources, which leads to the death and starvation of people, animals, and the environment, is <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5DTbashsKic\" target=\"_blank\">not good enough for me</a>.<br /> <br /> Edit: What matters most to me is that, when in my country we rise up against oppressors, and the US attempts to intervene (cough cough, Chile, Cuba, Middle East, Greece), they'll be US citizens protesting against those actions in Washington DC.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 04-29-2016, 04:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T14:25:59-07:00","id":878089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878050#post878050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Edit: What matters most to me is that, when in my country we rise up against oppressors, and the US attempts to intervene (cough cough, Chile, Cuba, Middle East, Greece), they'll be US citizens protesting against those actions in Washington DC.</div> </div> </div> </div>Might I add Vietnam and Iran there, as I believe the US was propping up some particularly awful people. And honestly, the US has a lot of blame to shoulder for helping give Osama Bin Laden a head start. Like, I'm not saying NEVER intervene, but it should be a VERY CAREFUL, VERY THOUGHT OUT, and NOT COMMON thing at all. Both the Democrats and Republicans have a huge number of hawks in power (Clinton being one of them).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878050#post878050\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I don't use any terms either way. Cisgendered does not mean normal in my book. My view of their normality depends on how they treat people around them, and whether or not they feel that they have to live at the expense of others (by denying them their rights, taking advantage of them economically, and so on). Accordingly, an abusive trans girlfriend clearly deviates from what I consider normal (hint: it's because she is abusive, not because she is trans - I've had crushes on trans women before)..</div> </div> </div> </div>On other things... I feel like using those terms has purpose in some ways when people are offended by true terms that aren't slurs in any fashion. If they're used enough, people start to get past the being offended and start to consider it (hopefully).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":378,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","user":"haren","date":". 04-29-2016, 05:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T15:17:13-07:00","id":878156,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>omenseer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang#post827135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> So, a number of folks here are active in their LGBTQ communities.<br /> I was wondering in your communities what is the non pejorative slang for people who are not LGBTQ?</div> </div> </div> </div>Straight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>omenseer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang#post827135\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I ask because I am writing a script and I am looking to this for some inspiration.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh lawd.<br /> <br /> If I were chatting with someone, and they used the term 'normie,' I'd probably give them a 'You wat, mate?' look.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828566#post828566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As a straight guy, I'm not wild about the connotations that &quot;heteronormative&quot; or &quot;cis-gendered&quot; are picking up. Particularly in places like the student protests at Brown University, they're being used as synonyms for &quot;misogynist&quot;, &quot;knuckle dragger&quot;, &quot;gay bashing&quot;, &quot;Neanderthal&quot; . . .</div> </div> </div> </div>Being heteronormative is no different than being sexist or racist. The connotation is negative because being bigoted is negative.<br /> <br /> As a lesbian gal, I've never heard anyone use 'cisgender' in an insulting manner. Perhaps transgender students at Brown are getting together and talking smack about cisgender peeps, I wouldn't know, but that's going to be less than 1% of the US population, so it's probably not worth worrying over.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=868973#post868973\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think there's a disconnect there between academic and general language.<br /> <br /> In an academic/demographic sense, our species is heteronormal: the majority of humans are heterosexual or heterosexual leaning along that spectrum.</div> </div> </div> </div>'Heteronormative' is not a biological term, it is not an anthropological term. It is a term created by feminist academics to describe <i>compulsory heterosexuality</i> in a society.<br /> <br /> 'Heteronormal' is a general language term based on heteronormative.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come and rock me Amadeus.","edited":{"userid":296,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","user":"hippokrene","date":". 04-29-2016, 06:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","name":"hippokrene","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar296_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2399"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T18:44:10-07:00","id":878317,"title":"","content":"Um... I didn't say biology, anthropology, or any other specific disciple? I said there's a separation between academic terminology and general terminology (though I don't know anyone that uses heteronormal as a general term so I'm not sure it really counts as general language). Who coined the term heteronormative (and most of those feminist academics did so as an anthropological term anyway) isn't really important to what I was saying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T20:58:22-07:00","id":878416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=828063#post828063\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious - what does adding &quot;Q&quot; (or &quot;queer&quot;) to the gay rights acronym add, conceptually speaking? I remember when it went from being BiGLM to LGBT . . . which made sense to me. The change reflected an increased concern for the rights/problems of transgendered individuals, and brought another group under the movement's umbrella.<br /> <br /> Is there a similar block of individuals, or set of issues, reflected in the new LGBTQ name? It just seems redundant, having both &quot;Q&quot; and &quot;LGB&quot; - sort of like saying the &quot;flying bird flew&quot; or the &quot;swimming fish swam&quot;. And despite asking around, no one seems to have a good answer (at least in my pool of contacts). If anyone has a moment, could you fill me in?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not sure when it came into being, but on one of my news/politics sites, someone did post a thread with a link to some UK newspaper that had the entire LGBTQRS (17 letters and their meanings) acronym. And some of them to me were just mind boggling as heck.. Such as what's the difference between someone Bi versus multi-gendered. Or someone Transgendered vs wrong-gendered. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fata-Ku</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=868543#post868543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">QUILTBAG has an unfortunate resemblance to &quot;douchebag&quot;.<br /> For a non-pejorative term for non-LGBTQIA+ I usually hear cis-het.<br /> As a cis-het, I'm fine with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have not seen the quiltbag designation before.. But i have seen MANY instances of LGBTQRSTUV, as people 'take the mick'.. <br /> For me i am male, and think that's all i need.. not cis anything.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>hippokrene</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878156#post878156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Straight.</div> </div> </div> </div>Na.. I;m &quot;Dat way, not crooked <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>hippokrene</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878156#post878156\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh lawd.<br /> <br /> If I were chatting with someone, and they used the term 'normie,' I'd probably give them a 'You wat, mate?' look.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where as i would more than likely ignore them. Just like i often do to people saying &quot;Hey you&quot;.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T21:03:19-07:00","id":878422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878416#post878416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have not seen the quiltbag designation before.. But i have seen MANY instances of LGBTQRSTUV, as people 'take the mick'..<br /> For me i am male, and think that's all i need.. not cis anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just &quot;male&quot; isn't enough though, as the question is &quot;how to refer to people who <i>aren't </i>LGBTQIA&quot;. You can be male and be gay, or male and trans and asexual.<br /> <br /> Heterosexual cis male is what &quot;just male&quot; actually refers to. Also &quot;just male&quot; is kinda heteronormative, as it presumes that everything else is a sort of add-on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:lucida sans unicode\">Bearer of the legacy of Trauma Bear<br /> Need a dice-roller? Check out <a href=\"http://bit.ly/dicemat\" target=\"_blank\">Dicemat</a>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/43-fata-ku","name":"Fata-Ku","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar43_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2240"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T10:29:57-07:00","id":878849,"title":"","content":"Which to me it IS..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T10:37:29-07:00","id":878854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Fata-Ku</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878422#post878422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just &quot;male&quot; isn't enough though, as the question is &quot;how to refer to people who <i>aren't </i>LGBTQIA&quot;. You can be male and be gay, or male and trans and asexual.<br /> <br /> Heterosexual cis male is what &quot;just male&quot; actually refers to. Also &quot;just male&quot; is kinda heteronormative, as it presumes that everything else is a sort of add-on.</div> </div> </div> </div>Everything beyond pansexuality is an add-on according to certain schools of psychology anyway, and I agree. I won't go around telling hetero people that they've repressed certain aspects of their sexuality with which they were born, but I <i>will</i> point out that their discomfort with others' sexuality is a defense mechanism and a poor one for that matter.<br /> <br /> (to clarify, what I'm saying is that we are all born pansexual but it tends to be suppressed as a result of early life experiences)<br /> <br /> As for gender identity, I look to Plato and Kabbalah (as an individual adhering to mysticism) and their view of duality of gender values in all of us. Again it is possible to repress one or the other, but my God (apologies for bringing metaphysics and occultism into this) encompasses both the archetypal, perfected Female and Male, and so does the ideal (as in Plato's world of forms) Human. It sounds like I hold these beliefs because I read them as doctrine, but in mitigating circumstances, I sought spiritualism compatible with my common sense, which doesn't turn me into a judgmental asshole.<br /> <br /> Anima and Animus, that sort of thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":59,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","user":"stylanski","date":". 04-30-2016, 01:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T10:42:38-07:00","id":878858,"title":"","content":"I can get behing that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T14:07:48-07:00","id":878996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878849#post878849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which to me it IS..</div> </div> </div> </div>But that is exactly the problem. Your concept of &quot;male&quot; is &quot;normal&quot; in a fashion that' alienates people that have different concepts of being male.<br /> <br /> Even if you mean nothing harmful in it, it perpetuates a social concept that people that do want to be harmful use to justify their actions.<br /> <br /> Those aren't add-ons, they're a more thorough statement of your identity. Sometimes you don't need to be thorough, sometimes you do.<br /> <br /> I'm an American of European descent. More specifically I'm half German, and mostly half Ukrainian (the Ukrainian side of the family's last name seems to be a modification of a French name rather than any Ukrainian name we can figure out, so there's a bit of French in there even if it was very dilute before leaving Europe). The German and Ukrainian aspects of my ancestries are not &quot;add-ons&quot; to the more general idea of being of European ancestry, there's just more specific.<br /> <br /> Sometimes &quot;male&quot; is all that counts - just like noting my European-ness is all that matters - such as using the bathroom labelled for males. If there's a gendered bathroom for males, it doesn't matter if your cis or trans, hetero, bi, or gay. You're male, the bathroom is for males.<br /> <br /> Other times the rest of your identity matters in a fashion that is either implied or explicit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878854#post878854\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Everything beyond pansexuality is an add-on according to certain schools of psychology anyway, and I agree.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find those schools of psychology highly suspect. There's very little evidence to this, and it perpetuates a lot of negative ideology towards sexual minorities. While science is science, and social repercussions shouldn't dictate facts, when picking schools without any solid weight towards one being clearly most accurate, I'm not backing a concept that justifies both &quot;corrective therapy&quot; that says you can be turned straight or gives fuel to conspiracy theories of a &quot;gay agenda&quot; to turn children gay.<br /> <br /> The biggest hole in this school of thought is that non-heterosexual orientations exist, and all evidence suggests that the numbers are relatively proportional to population. If we all start out as pan but are imprinted into being something else, how haven't heteronormative forces stamped out homosexuality? The existence of bi, demi, pan, and asexuality could be explained as individuals not taking to such imprinting, but the existence of homosexual orientations in strongly heteronormative societies doesn't make any sense in this model of psychology.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As for gender identity, I look to Plato and Kabbalah (as an individual adhering to mysticism) and their view of duality of gender values in all of us. </div> </div> </div>While I appreciate taking to these spiritual positions to find a mystic outlook that supports being a better person, I don't think it's a good way to discuss these things in general. Gender identity transcends individual - or even cultural - spiritual concepts. My personal spirituality doesn't sound that off from your own, but gender identity comes from people. My trans friends are trans because that's who they are, not because of my personal beliefs saying that the spark of the divine in all of us means that as the divine itself expresses the full spectrum of gender we all have the potential to express anywhere along that spectrum.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T18:03:45-07:00","id":879137,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878996#post878996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find those schools of psychology highly suspect. There's very little evidence to this, and it perpetuates a lot of negative ideology towards sexual minorities. While science is science, and social repercussions shouldn't dictate facts, when picking schools without any solid weight towards one being clearly most accurate, I'm not backing a concept that justifies both &quot;corrective therapy&quot; that says you can be turned straight or gives fuel to conspiracy theories of a &quot;gay agenda&quot; to turn children gay.<br /> <br /> The biggest hole in this school of thought is that non-heterosexual orientations exist, and all evidence suggests that the numbers are relatively proportional to population. If we all start out as pan but are imprinted into being something else, how haven't heteronormative forces stamped out homosexuality? The existence of bi, demi, pan, and asexuality could be explained as individuals not taking to such imprinting, but the existence of homosexual orientations in strongly heteronormative societies doesn't make any sense in this model of psychology.</div> </div> </div> </div>Here, weight is placed not on what sexual identity you possess, but how happy and comfortable it makes you. The point was that the only natural thing in sexuality, is that we are attracted to other people period. Sorry if I didn't make that clear.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878996#post878996\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I appreciate taking to these spiritual positions to find a mystic outlook that supports being a better person, I don't think it's a good way to discuss these things in general. Gender identity transcends individual - or even cultural - spiritual concepts. My personal spirituality doesn't sound that off from your own, but gender identity comes from people. My trans friends are trans because that's who they are, not because of my personal beliefs saying that the spark of the divine in all of us means that as the divine itself expresses the full spectrum of gender we all have the potential to express anywhere along that spectrum.</div> </div> </div> </div>Of course, I agree with you wholeheartedly. The point was made on a more quasi-philosophical, analytical level. That's what you get when you digest a lot of Jung, Plato, and Hermetic writings in only a couple of weeks... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.greece-bynight.com\" target=\"_blank\">Greece By Night LARP</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/59-stylanski","name":"stylanski","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar59_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"299"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T12:07:47-07:00","id":880334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=878849#post878849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which to me it IS..</div> </div> </div> </div>You say &quot;male&quot; or &quot;just male&quot; is good enough for you, when you're actually a cisgender male would eould likely think of a transgender male as a wholly different identity than your own. Unless there's zero divide in your mind between cis and trans people, claiming that &quot;male&quot; should inevitably mean cis is horribly marginalizaing transgender individuals, and is barely a step above calling yourself &quot;normal.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T17:45:40-07:00","id":880609,"title":"","content":"And since i am normal though, meaning i have no mental or other issues making me think i am something i am not, how is that disparaging those who are transgendered?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T19:04:46-07:00","id":880661,"title":"","content":"Because you're using the position of being &quot;normal&quot; by society's very biased standards to say that trans/etc. are not normal; which is disparaging because it is the main justification for discrimination against them.<br /> <br /> Look at your language, &quot;...think i am something i am not...&quot; do you see how strongly insulting that is?<br /> <br /> A trans-man is not a woman that thinks she's not a woman. He's a man with a body that society says doesn't belong to a man. You might not mean it, but you just phrased it as trans-people are <i>delusional</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T19:55:49-07:00","id":880681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>stylanski</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=879137#post879137\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here, weight is placed not on what sexual identity you possess, but how happy and comfortable it makes you. The point was that the only natural thing in sexuality, is that we are attracted to other people period. Sorry if I didn't make that clear.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that sandpoint carries the implication that asexuality is not natural. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880609#post880609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And since i am normal though, meaning i have no mental or other issues making me think i am something i am not, how is that disparaging those who are transgendered?</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:red\">I'm going to give you the benefit of the doubt and assume the implication that everyone who doesn't fit your definition of &quot;normal&quot; has mental or social issues making them think they're something they're not was unintended. But know that you are dangerously close to a serious sanction with this post. If you can't post in this thread without implicitly invalidating people's genders and sexual preferences, don't post in it at all.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T22:06:57-07:00","id":880741,"title":"","content":"Warning received Charlaquin. I will try to make my point without seeming like i am trying to make that connotation<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T02:37:12-07:00","id":880828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880609#post880609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And since i am normal though, meaning i have no mental or other issues making me think i am something i am not, how is that disparaging those who are transgendered?</div> </div> </div> </div>Being transgender does not mean you think you're something you're not, nor is it the result of &quot;mental or other issues.&quot; You and a transgender man are equally male.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T03:40:19-07:00","id":880849,"title":"","content":"Wow - this thread is still going strong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880828#post880828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(. . .) You and a transgender man are equally male.</div> </div> </div> </div>No offense, but when did anyone agree to that interpretation? I am male. I've got the Y chromosome, and the anatomy, to back up such a claim. I went through the male version of puberty, and can get a female pregnant. I represent myself as a man, and society recognizes me as such.<br /> <br /> A pre-op female-to-male transsexual - at best - meets only one of those qualifications. That's not to say that there's anything &quot;wrong&quot; with them, but I don't understand how you can argue that we're &quot;equally male&quot;. If I can get you pregnant, you can't really claim to be in the same gender as me.<br /> <br /> And, personally, I think this new notion of &quot;gender identity&quot; as being entirely separate from biological gender is really hamstringing the LGBTQ movement. Its just so alien to the way that &quot;cisgender&quot; individuals experience things, as well as traditional scientific definitions . . . A lot of LGBTQ advocates don't seem to realize just how radical and controversial a theory they're advocating<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <u><i>Edit</i></u>: For the record, I am not saying that the ability to get pregnant (or get someone pregnant) is the defining trait of gender. But it does stand as a very stark difference between the sexes, and thus makes a good example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4624,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","user":"One Vorlon","date":". 05-03-2016, 06:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T04:01:35-07:00","id":880858,"title":"","content":"But that's the thing, biological sex and gender are two completely different things. gender is an entirely social construct. for example, it is gender not sex which determines a person's role in society like saying &quot;men are more aggressive and go to work&quot; vs. &quot;women are passive and stay home&quot;. neither or those statements are anywhere near universally true for either sex, yet society tells us they are, hence the difference between gender and sex.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T04:34:46-07:00","id":880873,"title":"","content":"As well, sex is not nearly as binary as you present it as. I mean, in fact, do you know you are XY for sure? That there aren't any genetic quirks that might disagree with your assessment? Unless you've gotten your chromosomes and DNA tested, you could be wrong. And even then, you could be wrong. A lot more people are chimera than know their genetic quirks. And that's ok. But it's moot because you consider yourself male and so you are male. That's your gender and how you should be treated.<br /> <br /> In short, society and the laws passed in some places don't hold up against the science.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T06:28:43-07:00","id":880925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No offense, but when did anyone agree to that interpretation? I am male. I've got the Y chromosome, and the anatomy, to back up such a claim. I went through the male version of puberty, and can get a female pregnant. I represent myself as a man, and society recognizes me as such.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just out of curiosity, how do you <i>know</i> you have a Y Chromosome? Have you ever had your DNA sequenced? The sex on your birth certificate is the result of an extremely cursory examination during birth.<br /> <br /> Edit: Since this question has already been asked in one form or another, I'm gonna talk about biology and the human brain. Biology is <i>weird</i> and the human brain is a <i>pattern matching machine</i>. It is such a good pattern matching machine that it has two critical flaws:<br /> <br /> 1) It sometimes sees patterns where there are none.<br /> 2) It sometimes sees <i>inviolate rules</i> where patterns exist.<br /> <br /> Sexual differentiation begins about six weeks into the development of the human fetus. And the presence of the Y Chromosome in humans <i>usually</i> results in the development of testes, which flood the body with Testosterone and cause all of the other expected developmental changes in men. If sex is like architecture, the Y-chromosome is a keystone. You can build an arch without a keystone, and you can build plenty of non-arch things with keystones, but, typically, you use a keystone to build an arch. And when I say <i>usually</i>, I mean as many as one man in 100 does not meet your criteria for maleness before we even <i>begin</i> discussing Transgender individuals.<br /> <br /> For more information, I suggest perhaps you commune with Nature:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/news/sex-redefined-1.16943\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/news/sex-redefined-1.16943\" target=\"_blank\"> 303 See Other </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://www.nature.com/news/sex-redefined-1.16943\" target=\"_blank\"> http://www.nature.com/news/sex-redefined-1.16943 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":707,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","user":"etherial","date":". 05-03-2016, 10:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T07:29:37-07:00","id":880963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of LGBTQ advocates don't seem to realize just how radical and controversial a theory they're advocating</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, we <i>do</i> realize how radical and controversial gender theory is. But it's radical and controversial <i>because</i> it's rooted in science.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T07:54:59-07:00","id":880977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No offense, but when did anyone agree to that interpretation?</div> </div> </div> </div>1981, when Sandra Bem published her findings on Gender Schema Theory.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am male. I've got the Y chromosome, and the anatomy, to back up such a claim. I went through the male version of puberty, and can get a female pregnant.</div> </div> </div> </div>What you're referring to here are features of sex, not gender. They are biological features, whereas gender is a collection of social constructs. And these biological features are not even shared by all cisgender men. XX male syndrome is a thing. Puberty does not express itself in the same way for all cisgender males - some start producing more or less of certain hormones than others, some even develop breasts. Not all cisgender men develop anatomically the same. Not all cisgender men are sexually potent.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I represent myself as a man, and society recognizes me as such.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're very fortunate that the only thing you have to do to be recognized as a man by society is present yourself as such. That is not the case for many people, and that is tragic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A pre-op female-to-male transsexual - at best - meets only one of those qualifications. That's not to say that there's anything &quot;wrong&quot; with them, but I don't understand how you can argue that we're &quot;equally male&quot;. If I can get you pregnant, you can't really claim to be in the same gender as me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except the qualifications you listed are related to sex, not gender, and are even dubious as qualifications for sex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, personally, I think this new notion of &quot;gender identity&quot; as being entirely separate from biological gender is really hamstringing the LGBTQ movement. Its just so alien to the way that &quot;cisgender&quot; individuals experience things, as well as traditional scientific definitions . . . A lot of LGBTQ advocates don't seem to realize just how radical and controversial a theory they're advocating</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not really so radical if you actually do the research and learn what these theories are actually saying and the hard science they are founded on. I'm well aware that the idea that sex isn't as binary as most of us are brought up believing is difficult for many people to come to terms with. I'm also well aware that it's difficult for people to let go of misconceptions about the social concepts of masculinity and femininity being intrinsically tied to sex. But these are scientifically verifiable facts, and should be learned regardless of one's stance on LGBT issues.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><i>Edit</i></u>: For the record, I am not saying that the ability to get pregnant (or get someone pregnant) is the defining trait of gender. But it does stand as a very stark difference between the sexes, and thus makes a good example.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's an example of sexual dimorphism in the human species, and far from a universal one. But since social ideas of &quot;man-ness&quot; and &quot;woman-ness&quot; and their roles within society demonstrably have nothing to do with human sexual dimorphism, this argument says nothing about gender.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-03-2016, 10:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T09:35:35-07:00","id":881045,"title":"","content":"I find it a bit amusing that we asked the same question, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar707_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"707\" data-vbnamecard=\"707\">etherial</a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr />\u200bWhen noise turns to silence, when colors dull and pale, when reality no longer makes sense, there shall you find me. There, in the dreams of the River of Faceless Millions, do I dwell.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/\" target=\"_blank\">http://harenm.deviantart.com/gallery/</a> for my art.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/378-haren","name":"haren","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar378_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"664"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T11:03:40-07:00","id":881103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881045#post881045\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find it a bit amusing that we asked the same question, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar707_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"707\" data-vbnamecard=\"707\">etherial</a></div> </div> </div> </div>The amniocentisis performed when my mother was pregnant with me came up XX. I don't actually know if I have a Y chromosome (and the test was flawed), but I do have motile sperm...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T11:41:34-07:00","id":881122,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>One Vorlon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880849#post880849\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, personally, I think this new notion of &quot;gender identity&quot; as being entirely separate from biological gender is really hamstringing the LGBTQ movement. Its just so alien to the way that &quot;cisgender&quot; individuals experience things, as well as traditional scientific definitions . . . A lot of LGBTQ advocates don't seem to realize just how radical and controversial a theory they're advocating</div> </div> </div> </div>Another issue to me, is who came up with all these notions of gender identity and the phrasology Xix, cis etc?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DeeDee</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880858#post880858\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that's the thing, biological sex and gender are two completely different things. gender is an entirely social construct. for example, it is gender not sex which determines a person's role in society like saying &quot;men are more aggressive and go to work&quot; vs. &quot;women are passive and stay home&quot;. neither or those statements are anywhere near universally true for either sex, yet society tells us they are, hence the difference between gender and sex.</div> </div> </div> </div>Where as DD, i see sex being more of that determinator, than 'gender'. Since biologically men are more prone to be able to handle the heavier work and tasks needing aggression, more so than women...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>haren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=880873#post880873\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As well, sex is not nearly as binary as you present it as. I mean, in fact, do you know you are XY for sure? That there aren't any genetic quirks that might disagree with your assessment? Unless you've gotten your chromosomes and DNA tested, you could be wrong. <b>And even then, you could be wrong. A lot more people are chimera than know their genetic quirks</b>. And that's ok. But it's moot because you consider yourself male and so you are male. That's your gender and how you should be treated.<br /> <br /> In short, society and the laws passed in some places don't hold up against the science.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've heard that before (the bolded part), about a lot more people these days being Chimera, than used to be. Same with how we seem to get a lot more people coming down with Autism, or allergies. Which makes me wonder WHAT has changed to biology, that is causing those issues? Heck, when i was a kid (late 70s through 80s), we had maybe 2-3 kids in our school that were allergic to MORE than 2 things. If that.<br /> When i last visited my high school while on Leave from the Navy, one class alone had over a DOZEN students who had 3 or more allergies. One of the most severe cases, a gal was allergic to over 20 separate things..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T12:20:36-07:00","id":881166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881122#post881122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where as DD, i see sex being more of that determinator, than 'gender'. Since biologically men are more prone to be able to handle the heavier work and tasks needing aggression, more so than women...</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really, no. Like, technically, the average man is slightly stronger than the average woman, but the bell curves have 90% overlap. The ability to beat the everliving tar out of a person or take a foxhole or carry a hundred pound haversack or drive a racecar has a lot more to do with experience than genetics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T13:27:32-07:00","id":881220,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881122#post881122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another issue to me, is who came up with all these notions of gender identity and the phrasology Xix, cis etc?</div> </div> </div> </div>The notion of gender as separate from sex is at least 30,000 years old. While the language may differ, most cultures, including most ancient cultures, have some way of describing the phenomenon that some people fit into different societal roles than are traditionally associated with people who share their anatomical features. As for the modern form of gender theory, as I mentioned in an earlier post, a lot of it goes back to Sandra Bem's theories published in 1981. The World Health Organization defines gender as &quot;<span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">the </span></span>socially constructed</span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> roles, behaviours, activities, and attributes that a given society considers appropriate for men and women&quot; and that &quot;'masculine' and 'feminine' are gender categories.&quot;</span></span></span> The prefixes cis and trans, as mentioned in an earlier post as well, are latin and literally mean &quot;on the same side as&quot; and &quot;across from&quot;, respectively. Transgender and cisgender are both recognized by the Oxford English Dictionary and the Merriam-Webster English Dictionary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881122#post881122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where as DD, i see sex being more of that determinator, than 'gender'. Since biologically men are more prone to be able to handle the heavier work and tasks needing aggression, more so than women...</div> </div> </div> </div>That's just factually inaccurate. This is an old idea that has been long been scientifically disproven.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881122#post881122\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've heard that before (the bolded part), about a lot more people these days being Chimera, than used to be. Same with how we seem to get a lot more people coming down with Autism, or allergies. Which makes me wonder WHAT has changed to biology, that is causing those issues? Heck, when i was a kid (late 70s through 80s), we had maybe 2-3 kids in our school that were allergic to MORE than 2 things. If that.<br /> When i last visited my high school while on Leave from the Navy, one class alone had over a DOZEN students who had 3 or more allergies. One of the most severe cases, a gal was allergic to over 20 separate things..</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not that these things are occuring more often, it's that they are being diagnosed more often. In part because of becoming less socially stigmatized and in part because we're getting better at diagnosing them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-03-2016, 04:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T13:55:06-07:00","id":881244,"title":"","content":"Yeesh, you go to bed... and boom.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>etherial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881166#post881166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not really, no. Like, technically, the average man is slightly stronger than the average woman, but the bell curves have 90% overlap. The ability to beat the everliving tar out of a person or take a foxhole or carry a hundred pound haversack or drive a racecar has a lot more to do with experience than genetics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, even when there are genetic components to physical/learned skill aptitudes, people often forget about the other 22 chromosomes and act like our 23rd is the only one that matters. Things like height, or the proportional distribution of fast vs. slow twitch muscles, or visual acuity, or fat-metabolism, etc. etc. etc. are not all stuck on one freaking chromosome.<br /> <br /> You could have the best XY genetics on earth for being a superior athlete, and a trisomy 21 is going to pretty much ensure that doesn't matter.<br /> <br /> Human genetics are messy, and complicated, and predispositions are not guarantees of anything, and so on.<br /> <br /> We don't care about the 23rd human chromosome because it's more biologically important than the rest (we need all of them to survive). We care about what happens with the 23rd because it governs a large number of characteristics that human societies put large amounts of cultural stress on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T14:14:45-07:00","id":881254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>etherial</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881166#post881166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not really, no. Like, technically, the average man is slightly stronger than the average woman, but the bell curves have 90% overlap. The ability to beat the everliving tar out of a person or take a foxhole or carry a hundred pound haversack or drive a racecar has a lot more to do with experience than genetics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on my 20 years in the navy, experience counts somewhat i will agree. But pure genetics do for a lot more.. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881220#post881220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The notion of gender as separate from sex is at least 30,000 years old. While the language may differ, most cultures, including most ancient cultures, have some way of describing the phenomenon that some people fit into different societal roles than are traditionally associated with people who share their anatomical features. As for the modern form of gender theory, as I mentioned in an earlier post, a lot of it goes back to Sandra Bem's theories published in 1981. The World Health Organization defines gender as &quot;<span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">the </span></span>socially constructed</span><span style=\"color:#252525\"><span style=\"font-family:sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"> roles, behaviours, activities, and attributes that a given society considers appropriate for men and women&quot; and that &quot;'masculine' and 'feminine' are gender categories.&quot;</span></span></span> The prefixes cis and trans, as mentioned in an earlier post as well, are latin and literally mean &quot;on the same side as&quot; and &quot;across from&quot;, respectively. Transgender and cisgender are both recognized by the Oxford English Dictionary and the Merriam-Webster English Dictionary.</div> </div> </div> </div>Much obliged for that answer C..<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881220#post881220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's just factually inaccurate. This is an old idea that has been long been scientifically disproven.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is it? Let's look at the figures then. In my boot camp company back in 1992, we had 88 males, and in our 'sister company' 82 females. Even with the gals only being required to push out 18 push-ups to max out their Fitness tests, barely 1/4 of the gals could hit that max. For us males, 50+ was in the max territory, and well over 60% could hit that mark (i struggled but i have always had bad form for push-ups). In sprints, women often perform at a lower speed than men. Same with weight lifting.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881220#post881220\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not that these things are occuring more often, it's that they are being diagnosed more often. In part because of becoming less socially stigmatized and in part because we're getting better at diagnosing them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I will have to agree to disagree there. I do feel a lot MORE cases are being seen than used to be, but i will agree more are found out where as before they were ignored (or considered something else)...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T14:59:57-07:00","id":881281,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881254#post881254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it? Let's look at the figures then. In my boot camp company back in 1992, we had 88 males, and in our 'sister company' 82 females. Even with the gals only being required to push out 18 push-ups to max out their Fitness tests, barely 1/4 of the gals could hit that max. For us males, 50+ was in the max territory, and well over 60% could hit that mark (i struggled but i have always had bad form for push-ups). In sprints, women often perform at a lower speed than men. Same with weight lifting.</div> </div> </div> </div>This isn't data. This is anecdote. It's also the product of self-selection, and a huge amount of institutional bias. Military fitness requirements were designed to leverage human dimorphism as much as possible to deny women combat assignments and the promotion paths that are effectively only open to individuals with official combat experience.<br /> <br /> A sexist institution, using sexist tests, unsurprisingly results in what seems like data saying one sex is superior to another.<br /> <br /> When you look at, say, the Israeli military, which has had sex-based integration for a far longer period of time (and full sex-based integration for longer than the US as well), you don't see this sort of disparity. While the IDF has very few women in infantry roles (that number as increased drastically after a recent reorganization though, implying a lot of that was social pressure not a lack of ability), women actually make up (just barely) a majority of the officers in the IDF; and you don't get to skip physical fitness to become an officer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I will have to agree to disagree there. I do feel a lot MORE cases are being seen than used to be, but i will agree more are found out where as before they were ignored (or considered something else)... </div> </div> </div>For allergies, there's actually evidence for there being an increase in cases. We also have solid reasons as to why; which aren't genetic (see: good hygiene hypothesis).<br /> <br /> For Austism and genetic abnormalities that don't express themselves? You can disagree all you want. You're wrong. There's no evidence of an actual increase in prevalence. The increase in cases is clearly attributed to increased screening; with a bit of increase in the broadness of the definition of Austism that accounts for some of the increase there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T17:09:30-07:00","id":881366,"title":"","content":"Men are way stronger than women physically. I know this because I lift weights with my husband! We go together and chart our progress. I am FAR more disciplined, FAR more dedicated, and I always push harder. But I can't push even half the weight he can on some stuff! Other things, like squats, I handle just fine... but if it involves arms or chest, forget about it. And he's lazy as hell! I was truly surprised at how big of a difference it was! This may be anecdotal but believe me when I say all of the girls in the gym are aware of this fact. If you look at numbers for competitive lifting vs male and female you'll see the same stuff.<br /> <br /> That SAID, I really appreciate that RPGs allow me to play in a world where this is more fair, because it PISSES ME OFF in real life!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T17:30:49-07:00","id":881379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881366#post881366\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Men are way stronger than women physically. I know this because I lift weights with my husband! We go together and chart our progress. I am FAR more disciplined, FAR more dedicated, and I always push harder. But I can't push even half the weight he can on some stuff! Other things, like squats, I handle just fine... but if it involves arms or chest, forget about it. And he's lazy as hell! I was truly surprised at how big of a difference it was! This may be anecdotal but believe me when I say all of the girls in the gym are aware of this fact. If you look at numbers for competitive lifting vs male and female you'll see the same stuff.<br /> <br /> That SAID, I really appreciate that RPGs allow me to play in a world where this is more fair, because it PISSES ME OFF in real life!</div> </div> </div> </div>Different people have different body types, and some body types build muscle more easily than others. This is largely unrelated to sex.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 05-03-2016, 08:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T18:50:25-07:00","id":881404,"title":"","content":"Yeah, men and women strength is generally pretty close, though if you focus specifically on upper body strength it <i>tends </i>to skew a bit more towards males being stronger <span style=\"font-size:10px\">(though some of that is likely due to societal aesthetic standards leading women to workout their upper body less. And such standards might also mean that women with broad upper frames closer to the typical male reproduce less often to some extent. I don't have any data to back that up, though, pure conjecture)</span>. That said, the difference between a &quot;buff&quot; person and an average one of the same sex is gonna be greater than the difference between men and women at similar levels of strength training. Well, generally. There are many factors to consider - height, body type, prevalence of different muscle fibers, technique and experience with the exercises used for comparison and so on.<br /> <br /> And hey, If any female is feeling bad about their &quot;physical stats&quot; tending to be a slightly lower, don't forget you tend to have better flexibility ^_^<br /> <br /> In RPGs, though, it just doesn't really make sense even trying to portray any sort of difference between sexes. Not only are attributes accounting for too broad things<span style=\"font-size:10px\"> (like, to even <i>approach </i>realistic it would need multiple strength-related attributes, with interlocking influences between them and relationship to various resistance and speed stats, and would need separate scores for different body parts and so on, <b><i>way </i></b>too complex for a game)</span>, but also any difference that even could be concretely pointed out would like be too fine a distinction for the system's stat scale to handle. Certainly for 1-5 scales like in Storyteller/telling/path games, but even in the likes of D&amp;D or GURPS with the low double-digit scale for normal people, a one point difference by sex would likely be too coarse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>You need a picture altered to fit your Exalted character, or just looking for some visual inspiration? Check out the twice-reborn <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/266-photoshop-exaltification\" target=\"_blank\"> Exaltification thread</a>. And here is my <a href=\"http://danelsan.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Deviantart page</a><br /> <br /> S\u00f8ren Kierkegaard + Kim Kardashian = <a href=\"https://twitter.com/KimKierkegaard\" target=\"_blank\">Brilliance</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/868-danelsan","name":"danelsan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar868_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1492"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T22:20:00-07:00","id":881513,"title":"","content":"Heck, the ONLY RPG i know of which ever DID have a difference in scores for men-women, was 1e ADND. Where female pcs got capped as to what their max strength (without magical boosts) could be, some of them were a lot lower than their male counter parts (such as gnomes and halflings).. Though they did the Drow of the underdark as a counter point (women are stronger there)...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T22:21:34-07:00","id":881514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881281#post881281\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This isn't data. This is anecdote. It's also the product of self-selection, and a huge amount of institutional bias. Military fitness requirements were designed to leverage human dimorphism as much as possible to deny women combat assignments and the promotion paths that are effectively only open to individuals with official combat experience.<br /> <br /> A sexist institution, using sexist tests, unsurprisingly results in what seems like data saying one sex is superior to another.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>So saying a 25 yr old guy needs to do 48 push ups, 85 sit ups and run a mile and a half in 9 min 45, to get an outstanding, where a same age gal needs 18 push ups, 55 sit ups and 12 minutes on the run is ;sexist tests?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T23:48:25-07:00","id":881555,"title":"","content":"You're shifting goal posts a bit there.<br /> <br /> 1) Pushups (which are focused exclusively on muscle groups high testosterone individuals have an easier time building) are a horrible metric of overall fitness, and yes, the dominance of using them as part of fitness metrics is sexist.<br /> <br /> 2) You didn't mention sit-ups and mile and a half runs. I've seen plenty of women easily beat 85 and 9:45 of both. You mentioned sprints and weight-lifting. &quot;Explosive&quot; strength tests (aka ones that rely heavily on short duration and high exertion) heavily favor individuals with higher testosterone levels (aka are biased towards male sexed humans). High testosterone levels lead to increased muscle mass, which is good for such exercises. Individuals with lowers testosterone levels have an easier time building up muscle tone, which produces lower &quot;raw power&quot; but greater efficiency in removing lactic acid build up (aka is better for endurance).<br /> <br /> 3) That age gap is also significant. An 18 year old any human is generally not at their overall physical peak. They're not even done fully going through puberty (most people don't totally finish puberty until 20-22); even if most of their development is neurological at that point. It takes time to develop a high level of physical fitness. A 25 year old should outperform an 18 year old assuming they're dedicating equal efforts towards achieving a set of fitness goals. There's a reason why individuals that pass special forces fitness testing average in age ~30. It takes years of training to achieve (without going past the point of when the human body starts to down turn in physical ability). In most sports - especially ones considered highly taxing because they require a number of aptitudes - the average peak performance for professions is 25-30 years old.<br /> <br /> So yes, the fitness tests our military uses are sexist. This isn't even controversial. It's clear from military records that this was done on purpose when women in uniform were going to be allowed in combat zones, and it's not exactly something the DoD contests in their attempts to fight allowing women into combat roles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T00:43:41-07:00","id":881576,"title":"","content":"Heavy, in that last post i mentioned BOTH were same age..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T01:02:01-07:00","id":881585,"title":"","content":"Sorry, misread that bit.<br /> <br /> In either case, the idea that 25 year old women can't beat the male oriented numbers is ridiculous. My university's women's rugby team would have laughed at that.<br /> <br /> This goes back to something in my earlier post regarding self-selection though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T08:00:18-07:00","id":881792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881379#post881379\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Different people have different body types, and some body types build muscle more easily than others. This is largely unrelated to sex.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, take my word on it, this is NOT unrelated to sex. I am a total gym rat. I have many peers who are also gym rats. I go there, like, three to four times a week. I've been doing this for like five years! Any girl who has been lifting for any length of time is well aware of this frustrating reality.<br /> <br /> Dudes are stronger than women in lots of ways, but more or less the most amazing differences are in arm stuff (like bench pressing).<br /> <br /> I'll just leave this here! - <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Olympic_records_in_weightlifting\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_o..._weightlifting</a><br /> <br /> These are Olympic records, with the most well trained and carefully selected women in the world.<br /> <br /> And as a side note, try lifting if you are female! It's great! I've been doing it for years and feel much better than I did when I was doing 'girl' workouts. And you don't turn into a crazy muscle monster either, I still look slim and feminine!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T08:31:46-07:00","id":881822,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881792#post881792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh, take my word on it, this is NOT unrelated to sex. I am a total gym rat. I have many peers who are also gym rats. I go there, like, three to four times a week. I've been doing this for like five years! Any girl who has been lifting for any length of time is well aware of this frustrating reality.<br /> <br /> Dudes are stronger than women in lots of ways, but more or less the most amazing differences are in arm stuff (like bench pressing).<br /> <br /> I'll just leave this here! - <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Olympic_records_in_weightlifting\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_o..._weightlifting</a><br /> <br /> These are Olympic records, with the most well trained and carefully selected women in the world.<br /> <br /> And as a side note, try lifting if you are female! It's great! I've been doing it for years and feel much better than I did when I was doing 'girl' workouts. And you don't turn into a crazy muscle monster either, I still look slim and feminine!</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, you're talking about specific muscle groups? Yeah, hormones play a big role in that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T08:36:20-07:00","id":881827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881792#post881792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">These are Olympic records, with the most well trained and carefully selected women in the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>AKA the top .000000001%, Yes, the bell curve for men ends to the right of womens'. But if you lined up men and women by their strength, 90% of men would be standing next to a woman of equal strength.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/707-etherial","name":"etherial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar707_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"822"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T09:20:54-07:00","id":881890,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881792#post881792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, take my word on it, this is NOT unrelated to sex.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is correlated to sex, because testosterone levels are correlated to sex. As I was saying above, whether naturally occurring or artificially stimulated, it's high testosterone levels that actually dictates how the body responds to weight lifting exercise (building muscle mass vs. building muscle tone); though type of exercise matters as well.<br /> <br /> When it comes to the records you're citing, average body size also plays a factor (hence weight classes) because the moves in question (since it isn't just &quot;can you do this lift,&quot; there are specific regulations around form) are designed to have a biophysical structural limit where even if someone could get the weights up they couldn't keep the stance without sufficient body mass. The full moves in question require overall high levels of body strength, but the postures in specific are calibrated to high levels of upper body strength. It actually wouldn't be that hard to design weight lifting events that are more balanced between different body types, which would close the gap between the men and women in the same weight class in the current records.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Dudes are stronger than women in lots of ways, but more or less the most amazing differences are in arm stuff (like bench pressing). </div> </div> </div>Yep. Though again it's testosterone levels, not sex (though those correlate strongly) that determines things like distribution of muscles to the arms and pecs which leads to the perception that male = greater upper body strength.<br /> <br /> And, of course, this is still only measuring strength in terms of &quot;what's my maximum output in a small number of short efforts.&quot; If you take people that have all been weight training at roughly equal levels, and figure out what the maximum they can bench-press at high reps and high sets per unit of time, the difference shrinks fairly rapidly.<br /> <br /> Those big muscles that allow such impressive maximum bench presses suck at endurance because of basic physics. Their size makes it harder for the body's methods of clearing exercise by-products out, and replenishing necessary nutrients and chemicals, This is why marathon runners aren't muscle bound. Big muscles add weight that makes running more work, without adding endurance.<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> There's also the distribution of fast and slow twitch muscle types. This is explicitly not sex-related. A given individual's distribution greatly influences performance in various athletic events. A person with significantly higher than average fast-twitch distribution is going to cream someone with below-average fast twitch distribution at things like sprints. This transcends sex. You just don't see the impact of this when you do things like look at Olympic sprinters because they all have extremely high fast-twitch muscle distributions to get their in the first place. Likewise you can't be a world class marathon runner without hitting the non-sex related genetic lottery to slow twitch muscle distribution. And there's plenty of sports where a balanced distribution is more optimal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And you don't turn into a crazy muscle monster either, I still look slim and feminine! </div> </div> </div>So... getting back to terminology over biology...<br /> <br /> This is not helpful language. This simply perpetuates the idea that cis-gender women's average biological reaction to weight lifting is what a &quot;real woman&quot; should look like, setting another meaningless standard for trans-women to try to live up to for society to accept them as women.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T10:35:57-07:00","id":881961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881890#post881890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is not helpful language. This simply perpetuates the idea that cis-gender women's average biological reaction to weight lifting is what a &quot;real woman&quot; should look like, setting another meaningless standard for trans-women to try to live up to for society to accept them as women.</div> </div> </div> </div>All I can say is that when I try to tell people how awesome lifting is, what I hear from 99 percent of the girls is, 'I don't want to look like a weightlifter'.<br /> <br /> I actually think most men probably feel the same way ; IE, I don't think most guys want to look like professional body builders! They want to look good, healthy and strong... but not quite to the extreme that a professional does.<br /> <br /> Now, the issue is, most women I try to tell about lifting assume that you get like that in like... three months. So they're rather run on the treadmill every day. The issue is people vastly underestimate the amount of work (and frankly, steroids) that it takes to make you look like the people on TV who take this REALLY seriously.<br /> <br /> I actually think a little of that comes from gender role perceptions, though I don't know a ton about that subject. I think lots of my friends have the idea that if they stepped out of gender expectations, they'd suddenly sprout fur and howl at the moon... or in this case, get RIPPED AS HELL. In reality that kind of body takes astonishing amounts of work and (probably) chemicals. It's nothing to avoid weightlifting over.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3386-piff","name":"Piff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3386_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"442"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T11:35:57-07:00","id":882040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881585#post881585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, misread that bit.<br /> <br /> In either case, the idea that 25 year old women can't beat the male oriented numbers is ridiculous. My university's women's rugby team would have laughed at that.<br /> <br /> This goes back to something in my earlier post regarding self-selection though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't sweat it. I often misread (or gloss over stuff). But over the entire 20 years in, it was the rare case where a woman could compete with the guys equally.. and even rarer still that they would beat OUT most men. <br /> Now that said, i knew plenty who would beat ME out.. Especially on the mile and a half run! I have never been fast...<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T13:30:12-07:00","id":882172,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Piff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=881961#post881961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All I can say is that when I try to tell people how awesome lifting is, what I hear from 99 percent of the girls is, 'I don't want to look like a weightlifter'.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand. If this was a general fitness thread, I'd be on board with you. However, this is (supposedly) a thread about the language of the LGBTQetc movements. That means this is the thread to call people out on this like how their language follows heteronormative gender role enforcement.<br /> <br /> While many women (and you're right, men as well) overlook what could be very healthy and affirming exercises because of stereotypes around things like body builders, it's important to not swing back in the other direction. Saying, &quot;these exercises aren't going to make you look like something you don't want to look like,&quot; is a very different tone than, &quot;these exercises aren't going to make you look less like a woman.&quot; Even for cis-women who are naturally inclined towards becoming more ripped than average, this can be problematic because it gives them a negative body image as they're made to feel, &quot;not feminine,&quot; for having a muscular build.<br /> <br /> The intersection of how our culture uses body-shaming to enforce gender norms, and discriminates against trans individuals is an insanely stressful psychological mess to have to deal with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=882040#post882040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't sweat it. I often misread (or gloss over stuff). But over the entire 20 years in, it was the rare case where a woman could compete with the guys equally.. and even rarer still that they would beat OUT most men.<br /> Now that said, i knew plenty who would beat ME out.. Especially on the mile and a half run! I have never been fast...<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>This is why I go back to self-selection. The US military forces don't recruit women for combat roles (consider that we've barely scratched the surface of forcing them to allow women to test for them), so they don't actively recruit highly athletic women, while they have much heavy recruitment efforts aimed at athletic men. This is partially enforced by cultural elements such as very few women perceive athletics during academics as path towards a career so they have career plans outside of sports. Men are encouraged to see sports as a possible career, so there's a much larger group of male athletes coming out of higher education that don't make the cut for professional sports and don't really have something else they're dedicated to.<br /> <br /> Since military recruitment efforts are gender disparate in the first place, it skews their expectations and subsequently the tests they put people through. The military recruits women with an eye towards jobs that don't have high physical requirements (at least not ones they test for, talk to nurses about the physical stress they have to put up with...) because their idea for where to put women is in support roles. Men are recruited for combat roles (up until recently all combat role recruiting was aimed solely at men), which informs the physical requirements.<br /> <br /> This is the thing with institutional sexism. It's build down into the core assumptions of the institution. You can't just change one thing (open combat roles to women) and expect it to fix the whole system. You have to change the whole attitude of the institution to see women and men as individuals, and recruit them based on their individual strengths rather than use sex as a filter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T13:37:03-07:00","id":882181,"title":"","content":"Can the thread about queer terminology not be about anecdotal evidence of physical differences between the sexes?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T20:47:28-07:00","id":882504,"title":"","content":"to answer the op, straight is pretty much the accepted slang for heterosexual while cisgender (or cis for short) works for no transgender people, or people whose gender matches the sex they were assigned at birth. just remember that there is a distinct difference between the two, sexual orientation is NOT the same as gender identity. For example, a transwoman who is attracted exclusively to men and masculine projecting people is for all intents and purposes heterosexual.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5767-deedee","name":"DeeDee","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5767_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"116"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-05T19:00:42-07:00","id":883382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/827135-lgbtq-slang?p=882181#post882181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can the thread about queer terminology not be about anecdotal evidence of physical differences between the sexes?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b>Good point let's keep it on track.</b></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-